+### [3D Gaussian Splatting for Surface Reconstruction](17501-3d-gaussian-splatting-for-surface-reconstruction.md)
+Incorporates a neural implicit signed distance field (SDF) within 3D Gaussians for joint geometry and appearance optimization [17501-3d-gaussian-splatting-for-surface-reconstruction.md].
+
+### [On-the-Fly GS](17509-on-the-fly-gs.md)
+Enables robust online training during discrete image acquisition without spatiotemporal continuity [17509-on-the-fly-gs.md].
+
+### [View-Dependent and Appearance Modeling](17617-view-dependent-and-appearance-modeling.md)
+Replaces static SH color with physically based rendering (PBR) models or directional anisotropic functions for specular highlights [17617-view-dependent-and-appearance-modeling.md].
+
+### [Optimization and Training Loop](17791-optimization-and-training-loop.md)
+Encompasses iterative optimization procedures, including multi-view aggregation and subpixel jittering [17791-optimization-and-training-loop.md].
+
+### [Variational Bayes Gaussian Splatting](17968-variational-bayes-gaussian-splatting.md)
+Frames optimization as variational inference over GMM parameters, enabling continual learning without catastrophic forgetting [17968-variational-bayes-gaussian-splatting.md].
+
+### [BAD-Gaussians](18036-bad-gaussians.md)
+Recovers sharp 3D scenes from motion-blurred images by jointly learning camera trajectories and Gaussian parameters [18036-bad-gaussians.md].
+
+### [LiteTex-GS](18111-litetex-gs.md)
+Decouples geometry from appearance by assigning independent local RGB texture maps to each Gaussian, with progressive resolution allocation [18111-litetex-gs.md].
+
+### [Virtual Camera Optimization for Mirror Reflections](18121-virtual-camera-optimization-for-mirror-reflections.md)
+Models reflections as images from virtual cameras symmetric to the real camera across the mirror plane [18121-virtual-camera-optimization-for-mirror-reflections.md].
+
+### [Differentiable DOF Rendering](18289-differentiable-dof-rendering.md)
+Generates blurred images by convolving projected 2D Gaussians with depth-dependent blur kernels, enabling differentiable camera parameter optimization [18289-differentiable-dof-rendering.md].
+
+### [ParetoGS Continuous-Budget Decoding](18388-paretogs-continuous-budget-decoding.md)
+Returns parameters for exactly $K$ Gaussians at any budget by interpolating between nested adapted anchors [18388-paretogs-continuous-budget-decoding.md].
+
+### [SpotLessSplats (SLS)](18423-spotlesssplats-sls.md)
+Enables high-quality reconstruction from casually captured video with transient distractors using unsupervised outlier detection [18423-spotlesssplats-sls.md].
+
+### Sparse2DGS
+Uses sparse input views initialized with MVS points and geometric-prioritized schemes to prevent appearance overfitting [18424-sparse2dgs.md].
+
+## Operating characteristics
+
+*   **Time Complexity**: Rendering is $O(N \cdot M)$ where $N$ is the number of Gaussians and $M$ is the number of pixels, but optimized via tile-based rasterization to be effectively linear in practice [6233-optimization-and-parallelization]. Training time is dominated by the iterative densification and backpropagation steps.
+*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(N)$ per Gaussian. Standard 3DGS scenes can exceed 1GB for real-world datasets [12943-data-compression-and-storage-optimization.md]. Half-precision implementations reduce memory bandwidth requirements [S2].
+*   **Performance**: Real-time rendering (60+ FPS) is achievable on modern GPUs with optimized CUDA kernels [6233-optimization-and-parallelization]. Training can take several hours to days depending on scene complexity and densification steps [S3].
+*   **Strengths**: High-fidelity novel view synthesis, real-time rendering speeds, explicit representation allows for easy editing and compression [12943-data-compression-and-storage-optimization.md].
+*   **Limitations**: Sensitive to initialization, prone to "floaters" in unobserved regions, and requires significant memory for large scenes [15421-scene-representation-and-geometry-constraints.md].
+
+## Background
+
+3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S1]. It revolutionized neural rendering by replacing implicit neural fields with explicit 3D Gaussian primitives, enabling real-time differentiable rasterization. The method builds on earlier work in splatting and neural rendering, addressing the computational bottlenecks of NeRF-based methods [6233-optimization-and-parallelization]. Subsequent research has focused on optimization efficiency, compression, and handling dynamic scenes and complex lighting [S1-S19].
